1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,212
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,212 --> 00:00:08,110
Time to grade this morning's call — the part most shows skip.

3
00:00:08,110 --> 00:00:14,454
Emerson Electric, ticker EMR triggered and closed +0.7 percent above the entry.

4
00:00:14,454 --> 00:00:16,300
So far, so disciplined.

5
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:21,236
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

6
00:00:21,236 --> 00:00:26,645
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

7
00:00:26,610 --> 00:00:30,328
On the day, the book is down 419 dollars.

8
00:00:30,328 --> 00:00:38,218
State Street SPDR S&P Oil & Gas Exploration & Production ETF (XOP) leads, +1.4 percent.

9
00:00:38,218 --> 00:00:40,939
Alphabet (GOOGL) lags at -3.8.

10
00:00:40,939 --> 00:00:45,836
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

11
00:00:45,836 --> 00:00:49,363
On screen, the day's gainers and laggards inside the paper book.

12
00:00:49,319 --> 00:00:56,815
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

13
00:00:56,815 --> 00:01:04,231
12 positions, led by NVIDIA, ticker NVDA, Broadcom, ticker AVGO, Bank of America, ticker BAC.

14
00:01:04,231 --> 00:01:15,076
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

15
00:01:15,046 --> 00:01:19,996
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

16
00:01:19,996 --> 00:01:22,472
Every morning OROS proposes.

17
00:01:22,472 --> 00:01:33,345
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

18
00:01:33,345 --> 00:01:37,412
Inside the book today: 5 positions up, 7 down.

19
00:01:37,412 --> 00:01:39,798
Best, XOP at +1.39 percent.

20
00:01:39,798 --> 00:01:41,743
Worst, GOOGL at -3.84.

21
00:01:41,743 --> 00:01:46,870
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

22
00:01:46,870 --> 00:01:54,208
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -8.37% per trade over 176 graded positions.

23
00:01:54,208 --> 00:02:01,457
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 25% position size.

24
00:02:01,457 --> 00:02:06,761
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

25
00:02:06,761 --> 00:02:22,938
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 251 names close the day in Stage 2; 1 new invalidation queue exit reviews; position size stays cut to 25% until the record earns it back.

26
00:02:22,894 --> 00:02:25,146
Over to the sports desk.

27
00:02:25,146 --> 00:02:34,435
Day 27 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

28
00:02:34,435 --> 00:02:40,722
In first place, the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, at 103,787.

29
00:02:40,722 --> 00:02:46,258
Then the index fund, in gray, 1.2 thousand back at 102,566.

30
00:02:46,258 --> 00:02:53,577
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 3.6 thousand back at 100,159.

31
00:02:53,577 --> 00:02:56,204
The race has no finish line.

32
00:02:56,204 --> 00:03:00,520
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

33
00:03:00,520 --> 00:03:05,399
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

34
00:03:05,399 --> 00:03:07,276
Nothing is restated.

35
00:03:07,276 --> 00:03:09,605
Today's lesson is benchmarking.

36
00:03:09,605 --> 00:03:16,067
Any strategy must beat the boring alternative: the same money parked in an index fund.

37
00:03:16,067 --> 00:03:19,523
That is why the S and P line shares the chart.

38
00:03:19,523 --> 00:03:28,540
If the system cannot outrun it, the index deserves the money, and publishing the race every day keeps the system honest.

39
00:03:28,499 --> 00:03:35,834
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

40
00:03:35,834 --> 00:03:38,076
Nothing here is financial advice.

41
00:03:38,076 --> 00:03:41,064
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
